To achieve this effect in your React Native app, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a custom ScrollView component that keeps track of the current focused section and handles the scrolling logic.
2. Define a list of sections with their respective titles, contents, and tasks.
3. Implement a function to calculate the offset for each section based on its position and the current scroll position.
4. Render each section with a different background color and enable/disable the buttons and text inputs accordingly.
5. Add an event listener to the ScrollView to detect when the user tries to scroll past the last focused section and prevent it by simulating a bounce effect.
6. Trigger the state management to scroll to the next section when the user completes a task in the focused section.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet, ScrollView } from 'react-native';

const CustomScrollView = ({ sections }) => {
  const [currentSection, setCurrentSection] = useState(0);

  const handleScroll = (event) => {
    const offset = event.nativeEvent.contentOffset.y;
    const sectionIndex = Math.floor(offset / 100); // Assuming each section is 100 pixels tall

    if (sectionIndex > currentSection) {
      setCurrentSection(sectionIndex);
    } else if (sectionIndex < currentSection) {
      // Prevent scrolling past the last focused section
      event.preventDefault();
      setCurrentSection(currentSection);
    }
  };

  return (
    <ScrollView onScroll={handleScroll}>
      {sections.map((section, index) => (
        <View key={index} style={[styles.section, index === currentSection ? styles.focusedSection : styles.unfocusedSection]}>
          <Text style={styles.title}>{section.title}</Text>
          <Text style={styles.content}>{section.content}</Text>
          <Button title="Complete Task" disabled={!index === currentSection} />
        </View>
      ))}
    </ScrollView>
  );
};

