Here's a JavaScript function that determines which computer networking protocol is most relevant based on the input provided:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const sourceIP = inputData.sourceIP;
  const destinationIP = inputData.destinationIP;
  const port = inputData.port;
  const payload = inputData.payload;

  if (isValidIPv6Address(sourceIP) && isValidIPv6Address(destinationIP)) {
    return 'IPv6';
  } else if (port === 80) {
    return 'HTTP';
  } else if (payload.startsWith('Hello World')) {
    return 'UDP';
  } else {
    return 'Unknown';
  }
}

function isValidIPv6Address(ipAddress) {
  const regex = /^([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4}:){7}([0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4})$/;
  return regex.test(ipAddress);
}
```

This function first checks if the source and destination IP addresses are valid IPv6 addresses using the `isValidIPv6Address` function. If both IP addresses are valid, it returns 'IPv6'. If the port number is 80, it returns 'HTTP'. If the payload starts with 'Hello World', it returns 'UDP'. Otherwise, it returns 'Unknown'.

