To set up Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps, follow these steps:

1. Sign up for an Auth0 account and create a new application.
2. In the Auth0 dashboard, navigate to the "Applications" tab and select your newly created application.
3. Click on the "Settings" tab and copy the "Domain" and "Client ID" values.
4. In each of your React apps, install the `@auth0/auth0-react` library using npm or yarn:
```bash
npm install @auth0/auth0-react
```
or
```bash
yarn add @auth0/auth0-react
```
5. In each app, import the necessary components from the `@auth0/auth0-react` library and configure them with the Auth0 domain and client ID:
```javascript
import { Auth0Provider, Auth0LoginButton } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

const auth0Provider = {
  domain: 'YOUR_AUTH0_DOMAIN',
  clientId: 'YOUR_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID',
};

function App() {
  return (
    <Auth0Provider {...auth0Provider}>
      <Auth0LoginButton />
    </Auth0Provider>
  );
}

export default App;
```
Replace `YOUR_AUTH0_DOMAIN` and `YOUR_AUTH0_CLIENT_ID` with the values you copied from the Auth0 dashboard.
6. In each app, protect sensitive routes by wrapping them in an `Auth0ProtectedComponent` component:
```javascript
import { Auth0ProtectedComponent } from '@auth0/auth0-react';

function ProtectedComponent() {
  return (
    <Auth0ProtectedComponent>
      {/* Your protected content here */}
    </Auth0ProtectedComponent>
  );
}

export default ProtectedComponent;
```
7. Finally, ensure that each app has its own Auth0 configuration and does not share credentials with other apps.

With these steps, you should be able to set up Auth0 SSO for multiple React apps. Each app will have its own authentication flow and protected routes, while sharing the same

Here's the C code to convert `argv` to a C string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *c_string = malloc(sizeof(char) * (argc + 1));
    int i;

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(c_string, argv[i]);
        if (i < argc - 1) {
            strcat(c_string, " ");
        }
    }

    printf("C string: %s\n", c_string);

    free(c_string);

    return 0;
}
```

