To inject JavaScript into an existing webpage using your Chrome extension, you can use the `chrome.tabs` API to get the tab ID of the webpage and then use `chrome.tabs.executeScript()` to execute your JavaScript code in the context of that tab. Here's a simple example:

1. Create a background script (e.g., `background.js`) for your extension:
```javascript
chrome.runtime.onInstalled.addListener(function() {
  chrome.tabs.query({active: true, currentWindow: true}, function(tabs) {
    chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, {file: "inject.js"});
  });
});
```
This script listens for the `onInstalled` event and then queries for the active tab. It executes the `inject.js` script in the context of that tab.

2. Create an inject file (e.g., `inject.js`) with your JavaScript code:
```javascript
document.body.innerHTML += "<p>Hello, world!</p>";
```
This script adds a paragraph element to the body of the webpage.

3. Update your manifest file (e.g., `manifest.json`) to include the background script and the inject file:
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 2,
  "name": "Inject Script",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Inserts a paragraph into the current webpage.",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_icon": "icon.png",
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["inject.js"]
    }
  ],
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"]
  }
}
```
This manifest file includes the background script, the inject file, and specifies that the content script should be executed on all URLs.

